/*! @brief KeyObject class represents symmetric or asymmetric keys, each key exposes different functions

crypto.createSecretKey, crypto.createPublicKey and crypto.createPrivateKey methods are used to create KeyObject instances. KeyObject objects cannot be created directly using the new keyword.
*/
interface KeyObject : object
{
    /*! @brief Information about asymmetric keys
    The content of the returned result is as follows:
    ```JavaScript
    {
        modulusLength: 2048, // Key size in bits (RSA, DSA).
        publicExponent: 65537n, // Public exponent (RSA).
        hashAlgorithm:  'sha1', // Name of the message digest (RSA-PSS).
        mgf1HashAlgorithm:  'sha1', // Name of the message digest used by MGF1 (RSA-PSS).
        saltLength: 20, // Minimum salt length in bytes (RSA-PSS).
        divisorLength: , // Size of q in bits (DSA).
        namedCurve: '' // Curve name (EC).
    }
    ```
    */
    readonly Object asymmetricKeyDetails;

    /*! @brief The type of the key

    For asymmetric keys, this property represents the type of the key. Supported key types are:
    - 'rsa' (OID 1.2.840.113549.1.1.1)
    - 'rsa-pss' (OID 1.2.840.113549.1.1.10)
    - 'dsa' (OID 1.2.840.10040.4.1)
    - 'ec' (OID 1.2.840.10045.2.1)
    - 'x25519' (OID 1.3.101.110)
    - 'x448' (OID 1.3.101.111)
    - 'ed25519' (OID 1.3.101.112)
    - 'ed448' (OID 1.3.101.113)
    - 'dh' (OID 1.2.840.113549.1.3.1)

    For unrecognized KeyObject types and symmetric keys, this property is undefined.
    */
    readonly String asymmetricKeyType;

    /*! @brief For secret keys, this property represents the size of the key in bytes. For asymmetric keys, this property is undefined */
    readonly Integer symmetricKeySize;

    /*! @brief The type of the key, for secret (symmetric) keys, this property is 'secret', for public (asymmetric) keys, this property is 'public' or 'private' */
    readonly String type;

    /*! @brief Export the key information according to the given options

    For symmetric keys, the following encoding options can be used:
    - format: must be 'buffer' (default) or 'jwk'

    For public keys, the following encoding options can be used:
    - format: must be 'pem', 'der' or 'jwk', 'raw' (only for EC/SM2/Ed25519/Ed448/X25519/X448)
    - type: when format is 'pem', 'der', type must be one of 'pkcs1' (RSA only) or 'spki', when format is 'raw', type must be one of 'uncompressed', 'compressed' or 'hybrid'

    For private keys, the following encoding options can be used:
    - format: must be 'pem', 'der' or 'jwk', 'raw' (only for EC/SM2/Ed25519/Ed448/X25519/X448)
    - type: must be one of 'pkcs1' (RSA only), 'pkcs8' or 'sec1' (EC only)
    - cipher: if specified, the private key will be encrypted using PKCS#5 v2.0 password-based encryption with the given cipher and passphrase
    - passphrase: <string> | passphrase for encryption, see cipher

    When JWK encoding format is selected, all other encoding options are ignored.

    The combination of cipher and format options can be used to encrypt PKCS#1, SEC1 and PKCS#8 type keys. PKCS#8 type can be used with any format, by specifying cipher to encrypt any key algorithm (RSA, EC or DH). When using PEM format, only PKCS#1 and SEC1 can be encrypted by specifying cipher. For maximum compatibility, use PKCS#8 for encrypting private keys. Since PKCS#8 defines its own encryption mechanism, PEM-level encryption is not supported when encrypting PKCS#8 keys. For information on PKCS#8 encryption, see RFC 5208; for information on PKCS#1 and SEC1 encryption, see RFC 1421.

    @param options options for exporting the key
    @return returns the key information
    */
    Value export(Object options = {});

    /*! @brief Compare whether two KeyObject objects are equal
    @param otherKey the KeyObject object to compare
    @return returns true if the two KeyObject objects are equal, otherwise false
    */
    Boolean equals(KeyObject otherKey);
};